Credential site write permissions
==================================

Seeing a site does not authorize changing its stored credentials. A user with
settings:write at the role/API-key level can still have only a read grant on a
particular site. Credential create, update, delete and test operations now apply
has_site_permission("settings:write", site_id) before mutation or testing.
Read and unknown site-access levels are refused with HTTP 403; permitted write,
admin/full and inherited-null levels keep their established behavior.

Creation validates site ownership and visibility before its write-cap check.
Updates first check the existing credential binding, then check a non-null
replacement site's ownership, visibility and write cap before assigning fields
or demoting defaults. Moving a credential to a writable/global destination does
not bypass a read-only source site; moving a global credential into a read-only
site is refused as well. Tenant and invisible-site failures keep existing404
behavior and remain ahead of destination write-cap diagnostics.

Organization-level credentials (site_id NULL) retain the existing base-permission
policy. Administrator and zero-grant behavior remain unchanged. Site write caps
cannot grant a missing role/API-key permission. Credential deletion continues to
use the existing settings:write permission; this change does not invent a new
settings:delete permission. Read/list routes are unchanged by this correction.

Qualification uses actual PostgreSQL rows and HTTP routes with synthetic users.
Sixty-six cases cover each write route, source/destination moves, access levels,
tenant boundaries, administrator roles, scoped keys and organization-level rows.
Thirteen failures reproduce the old bypass;53 controls preserve existing policy.
Each case checks51 existing credential records. Independent application processes
exercise the same cases over real loopback TCP, with separate shipping gates.

The credential-test cases stop at an instrumented target-validation boundary:
permitted callers reach that boundary, refused callers do not. No DNS resolution,
secret decryption or outgoing vendor connection occurs. These cases establish
permission order, not authentication correctness or device compatibility.
They do not establish concurrent user/session/API-key/grant revocation safety,
change the zero-grant hybrid policy, repair historical rows, or qualify every
credential reader/consumer. No enterprise acceptance claim is implied.

The original private check also called the per-site method for a NULL site,
which was redundant with the route permission and broke two existing direct-call
TLS test doubles. The final guard applies only to non-null bindings. Existing
TLS assertions and user doubles are unchanged; the failure evidence is retained.
